    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d615">

                <pb facs="0853" n="773" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg773"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">647.003/326: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Sydney</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sydney</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-19T17:00:00+10:00">November 19,
                            1936—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 19—10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>All tariff increases tabled May 22nd have been temporarily validated until
                    December 1st. Meanwhile the Government has once again postponed the debate on
                    its trade diversion program<anchor n="33" xml:id="d615-f1"/> as it believes that
                    within the next few days it will be able to announce a satisfactory settlement
                    of its dispute with Japan.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moffat</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d615-f1" n="33" xml:id="d615-f1-content">
                <p>In his telegram of December 4, 1936, 9 a.m., the Consul at Sydney informed the
                    Department that the Australian Government’s trade diversion policy had been
                    passed through all stages by both Houses of Parliament (647.006/58).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
